WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.240
“I am having a fantastic time at PyData.”

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.900
“It’s been excellent, the talks have been

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.040
a mixture of academic applications and business applications.”

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.860
“I really love the PyData community,

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.700
and it’s one of my favorite conferences.”

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.420
“PyData is an educational program of NumFOCUS.

00:00:22.429 --> 00:00:28.779
We are delighted to work with local communities
all around the world to put on events that

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:34.090
bring together people who are developing these
tools as well as the users of the tools”

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:38.800
“I think it’s pretty amazing that PyData
is not only about the talks,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.930
it’s a lot about the people and the software.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.820
We are meeting here, just networking, sharing knowledge,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.580
and also contributing to open source projects.”

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.160
“I think what makes PyData special

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.500
or PyData London in particular —

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.920
is that it’s a conference and a meetup that is

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.280
from the community,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.480
by the community,

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.220
for the community.”

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.600
“I’m really excited,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.000
and I’m also really excited to see

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.340
how the community can really further my work

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.080
in the future

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.200
by giving me some ideas.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.640
So, the questions I’ve had have been really
thoughtful.”

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.970
“Nobody can innovate in isolation.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.700
You have to be plugged into the community.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.010
And that’s why things like the PyData conference,

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:21.010
where you can meet hundreds of exceptionally bright people

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.760
who are really keen on what they do

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.890
is something we support.”

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.320
“By sponsoring an event like PyData Amsterdam,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.920
we are able to give back to the community

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.440
and help to drive the innovation of open source technologies like Python forward.”

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.360
“We love sponsors.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.460
We love them so much.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.480
So...”

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.080
“There’s definitely something here for everyone.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.580
I personally have made some new friends,

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.560
talked to some really cool professionals, and

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.220
you know, it’s been a fantastic experience.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:45.640
I highly recommend you come.”

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.620
“I went to bed last night with a grin on my face

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.640
simply because I got home and just felt

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.500
happy

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:51.940
with the quality of conversation

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.280
and the quality of the people that I met all day

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:54.880
yesterday

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:55.700
here at the conference.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:56.920
It makes me happy.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:57.920
Yeah, it’s fun.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:58.480
You should come along.”

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.280
"We leave every PyData conference more impressed than the last."

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.440
pydata.org

